    <item>
      <title>Bit Flip</title>
      <link>https://Peilong.github.io/games/bitflip/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://Peilong.github.io/games/bitflip/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h3 id=&#34;what-you-just-did&#34;&gt;What you just did&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Computers store every number as a row of on/off switches called &lt;strong&gt;bits&lt;/strong&gt;. Each switch is worth double the one to its
right: 1, 2, 4, 8, and so on up to 128. Eight of them together make a &lt;strong&gt;byte&lt;/strong&gt;, which can hold any whole number from 0
to 255. The row of 0s and 1s under the number is that byte, written the way a computer stores it. So when you made 37,
you wrote &lt;strong&gt;00100101&lt;/strong&gt;, which is 37 in binary.&lt;/p&gt;
</description>
